WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.950
Hi, my name is Craig Halkett.
I'm the course leader

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.510
and developer for Hope for Recovery.

3
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.830
So, Hope for Recovery is a faith

4
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.870
to freedom recovery pathway,
which offers

5
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.110
individuals an opportunity to

6
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.300
explore faith as a recovery pathway.

7
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.030
We offer six 12-week courses,

8
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.350
which give individuals
an opportunity to

9
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.570
really explore these courses,

10
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.700
and each course offers
a unique aspect for change.

11
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.500
At the end of every course that we run,

12
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.630
people who do the courses
have an opportunity

13
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.430
to gain a pass or a pass
with merit certificate,

14
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.000
depending on attendance.

15
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.550
So it's a very structured course,
but it's also a fun,

16
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.570
friendly, relaxed course as well.

17
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.790
So in order to get a pass certificate,
individuals need to come to

18
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.030
six weeks out of the 12

19
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.000
and also get 50% in the end quiz.

20
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.590
And to get a merit,
they also need to come to 75%

21
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.510
of the group, which obviously is 9 weeks,

22
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.900
and to get 75% in the group quiz.

23
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.590
So once they complete all the courses,

24
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.600
regardless of how long it might take them –

25
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.700
sometimes people do it over
a complete two years

26
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.000
and they'll just keep coming.

27
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.700
Other times people might take
a wee break, then come back –

28
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.750
but once they complete all six,

29
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.000
we also have a final
graduation certificate as well.

30
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.630
Life before Hope for Recovery was

31
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.500
very different compared
to what it is now.

32
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.900
I didn't really have a plan
on day-to-day living.

33
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.500
I just kind of took it
an hour at a time.

34
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.990
I didn't really have…

35
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.100
there was nothing,
there was no structure.

36
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.600
After coming here,

37
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.600
it was the opposite of the life
I was living before.

38
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.990
I then got structure.

39
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.200
I got a wee bit more significance.

40
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.000
I had a wee bit more
of a safety net under me.

41
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.700
It all kind of started
way back in 2019.

42
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.500
I really just felt a stirring –
that's probably the best way I could put it –

43
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.550
to put together a completely

44
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.000
biblically based recovery group.

45
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.500
I really just felt by teaching people
some of the biblical principles

46
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.700
that could really help them
recover from their addiction…

47
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.000
So that's how it all really started.

48
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.540
So after we began a couple of groups

49
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.270
and we ran the material,
I thought this might be good

50
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.830
to roll out to schools as well.

51
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.790
So we managed to speak
to a few people we know

52
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.830
and we went down to
Vale of Leven Academy

53
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.400
to run a pilot scheme.

54
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.900
So we ran a four-week block down there,

55
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.270
where it gave people who come along
to the Monday group an opportunity

56
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.790
to tell their story,

57
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.230
and that then opened up
for pupils to ask some questions

58
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.600
and for us to answer.

59
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.300
Then the group material we've developed,
we broke that down

60
00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.100
to make it more age-appropriate,

61
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.000
and we ran some health and wellbeing
workshops with pupils as well.

62
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.000
A lot (laughs), to say the least.

63
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.630
It's taught me

64
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.900
loads about myself,

65
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.500
about how to deal with situations,

66
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.300
how to actually keep a house,

67
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.300
how to actually interact with
other people in healthier ways.

68
00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.800
And if it wasn't for the people
that run Hope for Recovery,

69
00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.590
I wouldn't be going to college this year.

70
00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.600
They helped so much.

71
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.000
It's a completely different life
I'm now living.

72
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.100
We really feel we've developed
a programme which could be used elsewhere.

73
00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.000
Hope for Recovery specialises
in community-based recovery.

74
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.500
So we're really hoping
that we can then train and equip

75
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.890
other facilitators and other
course leaders to take the material

76
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.500
out into different areas.

77
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.390
We'd also love… a number of people
who come to the group –

78
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.990
not all of them, but some of them –

79
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.300
will go on and complete
the full two years,

80
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:22.900
and I'd love nothing more for those
who are then going to graduate

81
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.500
that we could then help equip them

82
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.500
to go out and launch
their own Hope for Recovery group.

83
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.200
Many, many different highlights.

84
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.270
I think the highlight at the moment

85
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.950
probably has to be
our first ever graduation.

86
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.200
It was really quite amazing
to stand back

87
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.870
and see two of our graduates go on
and complete the full two-year programme.

88
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.500
That was actually quite
mind-blowing, to be honest.

89
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.400
Hope for Recovery has taught me things
that I can do on a day-to-day basis.

90
00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.800
It's inherently in me now.

91
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.700
It taught me that I can make mistakes
and the world doesn't crumble around you.

92
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.700
I grew.

93
00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.800
Yeah, it really helped me grow,

94
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.800
and I can't thank them enough.
